From: Pyra kernel work <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: ts3a227e: clear pending events at probe and resume

ts3a227e_resume() only re-enables the interrupt. An insertion or removal
while the system is suspended still raises INTB, and INTB stays asserted
until the interrupt register is read. When the TS3A227E sits behind an
edge-triggered interrupt parent, as on the Pyra where INTB goes to a
PCAL6524 expander, the edge happened while the interrupt was disabled and
is not seen again. With INTB held low no later edge can occur, so every
following plug and unplug is lost too, until reboot.

On a Pyra with the jack reported through the TS3A227E: plug headphones
in, suspend to RAM, unplug while asleep, resume. The accessory status
register reads 0x00, the expander's mic-int line reads low, the interrupt
count does not move and Headset Stereophone Jack stays on. Two more
plug/unplug cycles after that are not reported either: the expander's
own interrupt fires on each change, but the TS3A227E's handler never
runs, and mic-int stays low throughout.

The same happens across a warm reboot. The chip is not reset, so an
event still pending from the previous boot holds INTB low, and probe
only reads the accessory status; no interrupt ever arrives on that boot.

At resume, read both event registers to release INTB, then read the
accessory status as it is now and report it, before enabling the
interrupt again. Buttons pressed or released during sleep are dropped
rather than reported held. At probe, read both event registers before
the accessory status, for the same reason.

---
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/ts3a227e.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/ts3a227e.c
@@ -330,6 +330,15 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Clear any event left from before this probe. The chip keeps its
+	 * state across a warm reboot, and a pending event holds INTB
+	 * asserted, so behind an edge-triggered parent no interrupt would
+	 * ever arrive.
+	 */
+	regmap_read(ts3a227e->regmap, TS3A227E_REG_INTERRUPT, &acc_reg);
+	regmap_read(ts3a227e->regmap, TS3A227E_REG_KP_INTERRUPT, &acc_reg);
+
 	/* Read jack status because chip might not trigger interrupt at boot. */
 	regmap_read(ts3a227e->regmap, TS3A227E_REG_ACCESSORY_STATUS, &acc_reg);
 	ts3a227e_new_jack_state(ts3a227e, acc_reg);
@@ -352,6 +361,27 @@
 static int ts3a227e_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct ts3a227e *ts3a227e = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	unsigned int reg;
+
+	/*
+	 * A plug or unplug while the system slept raises INTB, but the
+	 * interrupt is disabled and, behind an edge-triggered parent such as
+	 * a GPIO expander, the edge is gone by the time it is enabled again.
+	 * INTB then stays asserted until the interrupt register is read, so
+	 * no later edge can arrive either and the jack stops reporting until
+	 * reboot. Read both event registers to release INTB, take the
+	 * accessory state as it is now, and report it, before the interrupt
+	 * is enabled again. Buttons pressed or released while asleep are
+	 * dropped.
+	 */
+	regmap_read(ts3a227e->regmap, TS3A227E_REG_INTERRUPT, &reg);
+	regmap_read(ts3a227e->regmap, TS3A227E_REG_KP_INTERRUPT, &reg);
+	if (!regmap_read(ts3a227e->regmap, TS3A227E_REG_ACCESSORY_STATUS,
+			 &reg)) {
+		ts3a227e->buttons_held = 0;
+		ts3a227e_new_jack_state(ts3a227e, reg);
+		ts3a227e_jack_report(ts3a227e);
+	}
 
 	dev_dbg(ts3a227e->dev, "resume enable irq\n");
 	enable_irq(ts3a227e->irq);
